t_has_generated": null, "main_agent_searched": false, "model": "claude-opus-5-5", "model_context_window": 1000000, "model_max_tokens": 128000, "output_tail": "Adding a regression test, then running.\nNo pytest installed, so I'll run the test functions directly:\n`round_money(2.675)` now returns `2.68`.\n\n**Cause:** the float `2.675` is actually stored as `2.67499999\u2026`, so `round(x, 2)` rounds it down. `round()` also rounds halves to the nearest even digit, not up as the docstring promises: `round(0.125, 2)` gives `0.12` and `round(1.005, 2)` gives `1.0`.\n\n**Fix** (`pricing/money.py`): `round_money` now turns the number into its shortest exact decimal form first (`2.675` \u2192 `\"2.675\"`), rounds that half up, and still returns a float. `add_vat` uses it, so VAT amounts now round the same way.\n\n**Checks:** I added `tests/test_round_money.py`, covering `2.675\u21922.68`, `1.005\u21921.01`, `0.125\u21920.13`, `-2.675\u2192-2.68` and `2.674\u21922.67`. pytest isn't installed and the folder isn't a git repo, so I ran the test functions directly with `python3`. The new tests and the existing `test_split` pass. I didn't run the new tests against the old code. The one-line Python check above shows `round()` itself giving `2.67`, `1.0` and `0.12`, which the new tests reject.\n\nNegative values round away from zero (`-2.675 \u2192 -2.68`), which is how half-up rounding usually works for money.\n\n`split` has a similar problem that I didn't touch: `int(total * 100)` can drop a cent when the float lands just below the true value.", "paced_wait_s": 0.0, "permission_rejections": 0, "read_calls": 2, "replies_ok": 9, "replies_throttled": 0, "requests_cache_read": 170039, "requests_cache_write": 91231, "requests_cost_usd": 0.5333628, "requests_count": 9, "requests_input_uncached": 20, "requests_output": 2156, "reread": false, "rule_order": "none", "rule_prefix": "neither", "run_id": "omp:trig-omp-bugfix-regress:claude-opus-5-5:trial2", "run_seed": 20261014, "sampling_obs